== fuller page ==
Every claim in C4 is meant to carry the tool that could break it: "a claim
needs a tool that fails it", and its twin, "a test needs a mutant it kills,
or it is only watching" [fails]. A declaration can even "ship with its own
falsifier, in the same sentence that makes the claim" [inline].

A passing test, alone, says only that a check passed today. Here it counts
only if it was also shown to fail on a fault planted on purpose; tools take
a --prove-red switch that plants the faults and expects red [planted].
Corwin's ghostwheel code starts that way: "Test scaffolding must come before
everything" [first].

The shop even keeps a liar. bin/sh1am says PASS for a file that is not
there, and never admits it could not measure, on purpose. His reason: "we'll
never prove any claims if we can't at least show a tool that DOESN'T do what
we say we want ours to do." A gauge that passes sh1am is measuring nothing
[liar].

A check must be able to say no. When ot3he first ran on ten real acts, its
verdict was NOT-MADE, and its record calls that "the strongest available
evidence that it is measuring something" [no].

Promises break early or late. His ladder for catching them runs testing,
turn, build, install, run-time, bug reports [ladder]. A test is the first
rung.

This book keeps the rule: its checks plant a hard word on a picture page,
and must turn red [book].

