== narration ==
# Take A, "the company talks to you": every voice speaks to the listener.
[picture A]
narrator: Here's the test bench. The little computer works, we say. But how do we know?
pin: Watch this. I'm pulling its plug, on purpose.
spoke: Look! The light on the test box went red. Do you see it?
narrator: Good! The test works. It saw the break.
spoke: Up on the shelf, that other box has no light at all. It can't go red.
pin: So it's only watching. We always break things once, on purpose, to be sure our test can see.
[fuller A]
narrator [fails]: In {C4|C four}, every claim is meant to bring along the tool that could break it. And every test needs a fault it can catch, or it's only watching.
spoke [inline]: A sentence can even carry its own check, right inside it.
pin [planted]: A passing test only says a check passed today. Here it counts when it has also been shown to fail, on a fault planted on purpose.
narrator [first]: Corwin's own ghostwheel code starts with its tests. Test scaffolding must come before everything.
spoke [liar]: The shop even keeps a liar, on purpose. It says pass for a file that isn't there. If one of our gauges ever believes it, that gauge is measuring nothing.
pin [no]: And a check has to be able to say no. The first time one ran on real work, it said not made. That's how we knew it was measuring something.
narrator [ladder]: Broken promises get caught on a ladder: testing, turn, build, install, run-time, bug reports. A test is the very first rung.
spoke [book]: This book does it too. Its checks put a hard word on a picture page, just to see them turn red for you.

# Take B, "the lap reader": one close voice reads to you; Spoke and Pin
# mostly talk to each other, and turn to you only in a short aside.
[picture B]
narrator: We say it works. How do we know?
pin: Ready, Spoke? I'm pulling it.
spoke: Red! It went red, Pin.
narrator: We break it on purpose. Then we see the light go red. Good! The test works.
pin: If a test can not go red, it is only watching.
spoke: Like that one on the shelf. Just looking.
[fuller B]
narrator [fails]: Every claim in {C4|C four} is meant to carry the tool that could break it. A test needs a mutant it kills, or it is only watching.
narrator [inline]: A declaration can ship with its own falsifier, in the same sentence that makes the claim.
narrator [planted]: A passing test counts here only if it was also shown to fail, on a fault planted on purpose. The tools call that prove red.
spoke [first]: Corwin's own code says it, Pin. Test scaffolding must come before everything.
pin [liar]: And we keep a liar. {sh1am|sh one am} says pass for files that aren't there. Our gauges have to catch it.
narrator [no]: When the shop's measure first ran on ten real acts, its verdict was not made. A proof that cannot come back no is a slogan.
pin [ladder]: Testing, turn, build, install, run-time, bug reports. I wrote his ladder down in order.
spoke [book]: And this book's own checks plant a hard word, Pin, to watch the check go red.

